Nope.  The idea is to have enough hit points so you are able
to run away.  Descend fast enough and you turn into a
popcorn instakill target rather than a character able to decide
to run away.

Level of monsters is based on the average of XP and depth.

If XP=depth then the monsters match you as exactly as the
assortment in the game allows.  But monsters average less
armor since you can collect more pieces each time your pet
shows an item isn't cursed.  And hostile monsters all fight
to their own deaths until you start to encounter named demons
very deep down.

So with XP=level you can kill any monster that won't kill
you, or fight a retreating battle against any that is stronger
than you currently are.  And because you have more armor
and more missiles there's a good chance you can kill any
one very strong monster as you retreat.

If XP=1 then monster strengths go 1/1, 2/1.5, 3/2, 4/2.5, 5/3,
6/3.5, 7/4 and so on.  By the time you're an XP=1 character
facing level 4 monsters at depth 7 you're a popcorn kill at the
first hit.  You can no longer run away because even one
monster between you and the up stairs can kill you.

No one seems to stay XP=1 all the way to depth 7 but the
principle remains.  With XP=monster-level it is possible to
fight through a few monsters in order to get to the up stairs
and survive.  Then with XP=lev-1 one level up it is possible to
fight the monsters that appear there while at the same time
surviving and healing to be able to descend again and survive.

Avoid leveling up and you can no longer retreat to heal.
He who runs away lives to fight again.  If and only if he levels
up to be able to have the points to to be ableto run away.

With geometric cost per level, XP=level stops working by
the time you finish the mines but it works fine the first few
levels.

Pets only supply just so much shielding from monsters.
And that's the other extreme.  Compete with your pet to the
point of keeping it form advancing and it gets weak enough
relative to the nearby monsters that it no longer attacks them
and so no longer advnaces at all.  It becomes a popcorn
instakill to the first hostile monster that happens to hit it
instead of you.



